  "summary": "The day after Bautzen, Napoleon pushed his army east after the allies, who retreated in good order behind a strong rearguard. Eugen of Württemberg's corps held the heights behind Reichenbach against Reynier's Saxons and Durutte's French through the afternoon, while Latour-Maubourg's weak cavalry and the Guard's light horse sparred with the Russian dragoons and hussars round Markersdorf. The rearguard got away; near Markersdorf a cannonball struck Napoleon's suite, killing General Kirgener and mortally wounding Duroc, the Grand Marshal of the Palace and the Emperor's closest friend.",
  "sources": [
    {
      "label": "French Forces, Battle of Reichenbach, 22 May 1813 (Nafziger Collection 813ECL, after Foucart)",
      "url": "https://www.napoleon-series.org/nafzigger/813ECL.pdf"
    },
    {
      "label": "Russian Forces, Battle of Reichenbach, 22 May 1813 (Nafziger Collection 813ECM, after Foucart and Fabry)",
      "url": "https://www.napoleon-series.org/nafzigger/813ECM.pdf"
    }
  ],
